{"name":"Rat Brown","hex":"#885f01","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rat","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Razajerdi."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the numerous members of several rodent families that usually have short limbs, a pointy snout, a long, hairless tail, and a body length greater than about 12 cm, or 5 inches.","A medium-sized rodent belonging to the genus Rattus."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rat"},{"term":"brown","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour like that of chocolate or coffee.","One of the colour balls used in snooker, with a value of 4 points."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a brown color/colour.","Gloomy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brown"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rat","blocks":[{"text":"Rats are various medium-sized, long-tailed rodents. Species of rats are found throughout the order Rodentia, with the genus Rattus containing the most familiar rats. Other rat genera include Neotoma, Bandicota and Dipodomys. Among the best-known rats are the black rat and brown rat, both of which are native to Asia. Rats belong to the same subfamily as mice and are often distinguished based on their size. Usually the common name of a large muroid rodent will include the word \"rat\", while a smaller muroid's name will include \"mouse\". The common terms rat and mouse are not taxonomically specific. Like mice, they are known to invade properties for food, water, warmth, and shelter. There are 56 known species of rats in the world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Brown_Rat_%28Rattus_norvegicus%29.jpg/330px-Brown_Rat_%28Rattus_norvegicus%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rat"},{"title":"Brown","blocks":[{"text":"Brown is a color. It can be thought as a darker, typically desaturated shade of orange, and can often be produced by combining red and yellow with another color, namely blue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg/330px-Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brown"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Rattus_rattus_-_Reid_Hardin_-_119483853.jpeg/500px-Rattus_rattus_-_Reid_Hardin_-_119483853.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Rattus_rattus_-_Reid_Hardin_-_119483853.jpeg/1280px-Rattus_rattus_-_Reid_Hardin_-_119483853.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Rattus_rattus_-_Reid_Hardin_-_119483853.jpeg/500px-Rattus_rattus_-_Reid_Hardin_-_119483853.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A black rat in Bee Cave, Texas","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARattus_rattus_-_Reid_Hardin_-_119483853.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Black_Rat_skeleton.jpg/500px-Black_Rat_skeleton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Black_Rat_skeleton.jpg/1280px-Black_Rat_skeleton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Black_Rat_skeleton.jpg/500px-Black_Rat_skeleton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Skeleton of a black rat (Rattus rattus) on display at the Museum of Osteology","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlack_Rat_skeleton.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Ratt_tail_detail.jpg/330px-Ratt